<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/intermediate/political-philosophy">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    States demand taxes, obedience and — sometimes — your life. What could make that
    <em>legitimate</em> rather than mere force, the difference between "the government" and "the
    biggest gang"? The classic answer, running from the seventeenth century to today, is the
    <strong>social contract</strong>: political authority is justified by some agreement — real
    or hypothetical — among the people who live under it. The method is the thought experiment:
    imagine humans <em>before</em> or <em>without</em> government (the "state of nature"), work
    out what rational people in that position would agree to, and treat the result as the
    standard real states must meet. It sounds like an odd way to argue — no one was ever in the
    state of nature — but it is the same move as the physics thought experiment or the economic
    model: strip away accidents, see what necessity remains. The philosophers disagree
    spectacularly about what life without government is like, and that disagreement decides
    everything downstream: whether the state may be absolute, limited, democratic — or
    unjustifiable altogether.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the contract conversation, 1651–1971 — order-first vs rights-first vs fairness-first" widget="timeline" config={contractTimeline} />

  <h2>Hobbes: order first</h2>
  <p>
    Writing during the English Civil War, Hobbes built the argument like a geometric proof in
    <em>Leviathan</em> (1651). Premise one: humans are roughly equal in vulnerability — the
    weakest can kill the strongest, by stealth or alliance. Premise two: resources are scarce and
    we are competitive, so we anticipate attack and strike first (pre-emptive "diffidence").
    Conclusion: without a common power to overawe everyone, life becomes "solitary, poor,
    nasty, brutish, and short" — a war of all against all, with "no arts; no letters; no society;
    and which is worst of all, continual fear". Rational people in that position would trade
    nearly everything for security: authorise an <strong>absolute sovereign</strong> whose
    decisions everyone treats as law, because any weaker arrangement reopens the war. Note the
    audacity: Hobbes founds the state not on divine right or tradition but on self-interest
    rationally calculated — the first fully secular theory of political obligation.
  </p>
  <p>
    The standard objection writes itself: if the sovereign becomes the predator, you have
    exchanged anarchy for tyranny with no appeal. Hobbes bit that bullet — anything, he said, is
    better than the war — and few have followed him all the way down. But his deeper premise,
    that humans are individuals first and citizens by contract, became the shared starting point
    of the entire modern tradition. Even his opponents argue on his terrain.
  </p>

  <h2>Locke: rights first</h2>
  <p>
    Locke's <em>Two Treatises</em> (1689) accepts Hobbes's method and rejects his anthropology.
    The state of nature is not a war: it already has law — the <strong>law of nature</strong>,
    discoverable by reason, which forbids anyone from harming another in "life, health, liberty
    or possessions". People own themselves and own the fruits of mixing their labour with the
    world; the inconvenience of the state of nature is not violence but <em>bias</em> — everyone
    judges their own case. Government is therefore created for a specific job: impartial
    enforcement of pre-existing rights. Its authority is <strong>limited and conditional</strong> —
    and when rulers become predators, the contract is void and rebellion is not merely permitted
    but a duty. That argument, copied nearly verbatim by Jefferson into the Declaration of
    Independence, is the intellectual ammunition of 1688 and 1776 alike.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> Locke's "state of nature" means a free-for-all with no rules.
  It has rules — natural law — and even property. What it lacks is a neutral judge and a
  reliable enforcer. The state, in this picture, is not the source of your rights but the
  <em>security service</em> for rights you had all along. Modern "rights exist prior to
  government" talk descends from exactly this.</p>

  <h2>Rousseau: the people as sovereign</h2>
  <p>
    Rousseau's <em>The Social Contract</em> (1762) opens with the tradition's most famous
    sentence: "Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains." His diagnosis was that
    legitimacy has been counterfeited — most states rest on conquest dressed as agreement. In the
    <em>Discourse on Inequality</em> (1755) he had argued that property was the original sin: the
    first person who fenced land and said "this is mine" founded modern misery. The cure is not
    a sovereign above the people but the people <em>as</em> sovereign: each citizen gives
    themselves wholly to the community and receives, through the <strong>general will</strong>,
    a share in lawmaking — laws that aim at the common good rather than any faction. The will of
    all (what everyone happens to want) differs from the general will (what serves everyone as
    citizen), and majorities can be wrong about it. Rousseau's vocabulary — sovereignty of the
    people, the nation as the source of law — fuelled the French Revolution and every movement
    since that claims to speak for "the people".
  </p>
  <p>
    The danger is visible in his own phrase that dissenters may be "forced to be free": if the
    general will is real but interpretive, whoever claims to interpret it claims unlimited
    power. The twentieth century's people's democracies made the ambiguity lethal. Later
    contract theorists, Rawls above all, are partly an attempt to keep Rousseau's democratic
    insight while removing the interpreter's blank cheque.
  </p>

  <h2>Mill: where liberty ends</h2>
  <p>
    By 1859 the question had shifted from <em>who may rule</em> to <em>how far ruling
    reaches</em>. Mill's <em>On Liberty</em> supplies the modern boundary: "the only purpose for
    which power can be rightfully exercised over any member of a civilized community, against his
    will, is to prevent harm to others." Over <em>himself</em>, over his own body and mind, the
    individual is sovereign. The <strong>harm principle</strong> answers the majority's
    temptation to legislate its own tastes — Mill called this "the tyranny of the majority",
    and defended it even for opinions he found hateful, because suppressed truth becomes dead
    dogma and suppression usually turns out to be suppression of part of the truth. His
    arguments are not contract-theoretic in the strict sense, but they complete the tradition's
    arc: from justifying the state to fencing it.
  </p>

  <h2>Rawls: the contract rebuilt as fairness</h2>
  <p>
    A century of utilitarian arithmetic — maximise total happiness, whoever bears the cost —
    left justice looking like bookkeeping that could justify sacrificing anyone. Rawls's
    <em>A Theory of Justice</em> (1971) rebuilt the contract with a sharper hypothetical: design
    society's basic rules <strong>behind a "veil of ignorance"</strong>, not knowing who you will
    be in it — your class, gender, health, talents, homeland, all hidden.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="Rawls's veil of ignorance — the contract argued in four steps" widget="equationStep" config={veilCfg} />

  <p>
    Behind the veil, rational caution protects the position you might occupy — the poorest, the
    least powerful. The rules that survive, Rawls argued, are two. First, each person has an
    <strong>equal claim to basic liberties</strong> (conscience, speech, person, political
    participation) that cannot be traded for economic gains — liberty has lexical priority.
    Second, social and economic inequalities are permissible only if attached to offices open to
    all under fair opportunity <em>and</em> they benefit the least advantaged — the
    <strong>difference principle</strong>. Notice what the principle permits and forbids: it
    allows the surgeon to out-earn the cleaner, because higher pay recruits talent that then
    improves everyone's healthcare; it forbids inequalities that merely reflect inherited
    position. Rawls turned the contract from a story about state origins into a test for
    institutions, and the "veil" is now the standard device wherever policy is argued —
    including in arguments about AI systems and their rules.
  </p>
  <p>
    The backlash came fast and stays instructive. Robert Nozick (<em>Anarchy, State, and
    Utopia</em>, 1974) accepted the method and reversed the verdict: taxation to fund
    redistribution is "on a par with forced labour", because people own their talents and what
    they produce; the legitimate state is a minimal night-watchman. Communitarians (Michael
    Sandel, Alasdair MacIntyre) attacked the premise: no one reasons from nowhere — we are
    constituted by our communities, languages and histories, and a self behind the veil is a
    fiction. Both critiques sharpened the position they attacked: the debate you can still
    overhear about welfare, tax and markets is largely Rawls versus Nozick with new examples.
  </p>

  <h2>The standing objections</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No one signed.</strong> Hume's 1753 essay "Of the Original Contract" is still the
    cleanest statement: no historical contract exists — most states were founded on conquest,
    and consent "to be hanged" under threat is no consent. Defenders answer that the contract is
    a <em>test</em> of legitimacy, not a historical event: the question is what free and equal
    people <em>could</em> agree to, and actual states measure badly against it. Whether a
    hypothetical can generate real obligation is the deepest open wound in the tradition.</li>
    <li><strong>Who counts as "the people"?</strong> The contract's "we" was historically narrow.
    Carole Pateman (<em>The Sexual Contract</em>, 1988) showed the classic theorists assumed a
    male contractor whose "natural" subordination of women predated and survived the contract;
    Charles Mills (<em>The Racial Contract</em>, 1997) argued the agreement was in practice
    between whites, policing an empire of the excluded. Both read the tradition as having built
    exclusions into its "universal" person — and both force the modern veil to be checked for
    its own blind spots.</li>
    <li><strong>Consent fades.</strong> If legitimacy comes from agreement, what about citizens
    born into the deal? Every generation is, in Hume's phrase, "months old" at contract time.
    Answers split: tacit consent (you consented by staying — but where could you go?); fair play
    (Hart, Rawls: if you accept the scheme's benefits, you owe compliance); and the associative
    view (membership itself obliges, like a family you didn't choose). None is fully
    satisfying; together they explain why the obligation question never quite closes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Plato wrote the first draft.</strong> The <em>Crito</em> (c. 399 BC) stages the
    whole debate in a prison: Socrates, offered escape, hears the Laws of Athens argue that he
    has accepted the city's benefits for seventy years and owes it obedience even to death.
    Socrates agrees. It is the first recorded argument from fair play — and the first
    acknowledgment that the contract, whatever it is, binds hardest exactly when it costs you.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The contract meets game theory.</strong> Twentieth-century theorists formalised
    Hobbes's problem: the state of nature is a repeated prisoner's dilemma, cooperation is
    rational only with enforcement, and the sovereign is the enforcement mechanism (Gauthier's
    <em>Morals by Agreement</em>, 1986). Evolutionary accounts (Skyrms, <em>The Stag Hunt</em>)
    ask whether norms of cooperation could emerge without any contract at all — which, if true,
    softens Hobbes's premise and, with it, the argument for absolute authority. The mathematics
    does not settle the philosophy, but it exposes exactly which premises are doing the work.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Philosophical anarchism: refusing the question's premise.</strong> A. John Wolff
    (<em>In Defense of Anarchism</em>, 1970) argued that authority and autonomy are flatly
    incompatible: to be autonomous is to be self-governing, so obeying "because the state says
    so" can never be right — you obey laws only when your own judgment endorses them. Few
    follow Wolff into anarchism; many accept his middle conclusion: the state's claim to
    authority is always in need of justification, never automatic. That inversion — the burden
    of proof on power — may be the tradition's most durable bequest.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>East of the contract.</strong> The contract tradition assumes individuals who
    negotiate; Confucian political thought assumes <em>roles</em> — ruler and subject, parent
    and child — with duties flowing both ways and legitimacy earned by moral example rather
    than agreement (Mencius went so far as to license deposing tyrants, arriving at Locke's
    conclusion by a different road). Vedantic and Buddhist thought question the boundaries of
    the self that signs any contract at all. Reading the two traditions together is a good test
    of the contract's own hidden premise: that political life is a deal struck among separate
    selves, rather than a web of relationships one is born into.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Why it still matters.</strong> Pandemic lockdowns, compulsory taxation, climate
    obligations to people not yet born — every one is a contract argument conducted at speed.
    The lockdown debates of 2020 replayed Hobbes versus Locke almost verbatim: how much liberty
    against how much protection, and who decides. When constitutions are written or rewritten,
    when bills of rights are litigated, when a new technology asks "who is responsible?", the
    questions are always the same three: what would free and equal people agree to? who was
    left out of the "we"? and what do we owe those who never signed? The tradition's genius is
    not that it answers them but that it makes refusing to answer impossible.
  </p>
